<div class="sop-meta">
  <div><strong>Trigger</strong> 30 days post-launch; after a major update; after a save (emergency-resolved or migration completed)</div>
  <div><strong>Owner</strong> Jon</div>
  <div><strong>Time</strong> 10 min per ask, ~1 hour/quarter aggregating</div>
  <div><strong>Output</strong> Google review collected + permission to publish on the site</div>
</div>

<h2>When to ask</h2>
<p>Three windows produce nearly all reviews. Outside these, the ask is awkward and the conversion rate is low.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>30 days post-launch.</strong> Client has had a month with the site, the launch was fresh enough to recall, the analytics dashboard has shown real traffic. Single highest-converting moment.</li>
  <li><strong>After a major update.</strong> Visible feature or improvement that the client notices. Ask within a week of the update.</li>
  <li><strong>After a save.</strong> Emergency resolved, migration completed, problem fixed quickly. The client is in active gratitude mode; ask same day.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Channel mix</h2>
<p>Always ask for the Google Business Profile review first, it is the highest-leverage signal for local SEO. Layer the others in.</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Google Business Profile review</strong>, first ask, every time.</li>
  <li><strong>Written testimonial for the site (<code>/testimonial/</code>)</strong>, second ask, ~2 weeks after the Google review. Used on case studies + the (currently hidden) testimonials page when it un-hides.</li>
  <li><strong>Video clip (Loom or phone)</strong>, bonus ask. Optional, only if the client is comfortable on camera. 60-90 seconds, three questions.</li>
  <li><strong>LinkedIn recommendation</strong>, for owner-to-owner social proof. Lowest priority; only ask when the client is also a connection.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Scripts</h2>

<h3>Ask 1: 30-day post-launch (email)</h3>
<pre>Subject: Quick favor at the 30-day mark

Hi {first_name},

Hard to believe it has already been a month since {site_name} went live.
A quick check on the numbers: {one specific stat from the analytics
dashboard, e.g. "you crossed 400 mobile visits last week, almost
double the pre-launch month"}.

If the experience of working with me was a positive one, the single
biggest thing you could do to help me is leave a Google review for
Pikes Peak Web Designs. Two minutes, three sentences:

{Google review link}

If anything has been less than great, tell me first and I will fix it
before anyone else sees it.

— Jon</pre>

<h3>Ask 2: written testimonial (email, ~2 weeks after the Google review)</h3>
<pre>Subject: One more small ask

Hi {first_name},

Thank you for the Google review, it genuinely matters.

If you have 5 minutes, would you be willing to fill out the
testimonial form? It is structured so you can answer 4 short questions
instead of writing a paragraph from scratch.

{link to /testimonial/}

If you would rather not, ignore this and we are still good.

— Jon</pre>

<h2>Negative review response policy</h2>
<p>The plan in advance, before the moment arrives:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Acknowledge publicly within 24 hours.</strong> Short, professional, no defensiveness.</li>
  <li><strong>Offer to take it private to fix it.</strong> "I would like to make this right, can you email me at [address]?"</li>
  <li><strong>Fix the underlying problem.</strong> Whatever it was, address it. Update the SOP if needed.</li>
  <li><strong>Ask for the review to be updated only after the fix.</strong> Do not ask for it to be removed; ask if the client would consider editing it now that the issue is resolved.</li>
  <li><strong>Never argue with a customer in public.</strong> The audience is everyone who reads the review later, not the reviewer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tracking</h2>
<p>Maintain a simple sheet. Columns:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Client name, project name, launch date.</li>
  <li>Ask 1 (Google review) sent? Date? Outcome (review left / no response / declined).</li>
  <li>Ask 2 (testimonial) sent? Date? Outcome.</li>
  <li>Permission to publish testimonial? (Y/N)</li>
  <li>Star rating + review excerpt for the GBP.</li>
</ul>

<h2>KPIs</h2>
<ul>
  <li>% of launched clients who leave a Google review within 60 days. Target: 80%+.</li>
  <li>Average star rating: maintain 4.9+.</li>
  <li>Review velocity: at least one new review per active month, even when not actively launching.</li>
  <li>Testimonials with publish permission: at least 1 per quarter.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common issues</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Asking too late.</strong> 60+ days post-launch and the review rate falls off a cliff. Ask at 30 days; do not slip.</li>
  <li><strong>One ask, one channel, no follow-up.</strong> Two-touch sequence (Google then written) doubles the testimonial yield.</li>
  <li><strong>Generic ask language.</strong> Specific reference to their site, their numbers, their use case. Specificity converts.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Un-hide trigger for /testimonials/</h2>
<p>The <code>/testimonials/</code> page is currently noindex / robots-disallow / unlinked. Trigger to un-hide:</p>
<ul>
  <li>1 published testimonial &rarr; add a footer link, keep noindex.</li>
  <li>3 published testimonials &rarr; remove noindex, surface in nav, link from homepage.</li>
</ul>
